 "limitations": {
  "extent": "Each 5 m/pix site DEM is 16 km x 16 km, so the ray-march radius is limited to the distance from the site to the nearest edge (6-9 km). Terrain beyond that - including the massifs and crater walls that matter at 20-100 km - is NOT in these files.",
  "resolution": "5 m/pixel horizontal, ~0.3 m RMS height uncertainty (Barker et al. 2021).",
  "observer_height": "The obstruction angles here use a ground-level (0 m) observer. The Phase 3 horizon masks sample the same skyline from the 2 m eye used for the Sun/Earth track, which lowers near-field angles by atan(2 m / distance) - up to ~8 deg for a rim 15 m away. Use the masks, not these summary angles, for visibility.",
  "wider_context": "For horizons beyond ~9 km use a wider LOLA polar product in the same stereographic frame, e.g. LDEM_875S_20M (20 m/pix, south of 87.5 S) from the PDS Geosciences Node, and record it here before quoting angles from it.",
  "singularities": "Heights are relative to the 1737.4 km sphere, not to a geoid (the Moon has none). Slope and horizon angles assume local north points away from the pole; the pole itself is excluded (azimuth undefined).",
  "gaps": "NaN marks no-data cells at the raster edges; they are counted, never filled with zero.",
  "display_layers": "Moon Trek LOLA tiles are 8-bit display imagery: they confirm layer coverage at the site and are never used for heights or angles.",
  "scope": "Terrain gives geometric obstruction only. It does not model lander height beyond the recorded observer, rocks, thermal conditions or landing hazards, and a clear skyline is not a landing certification."
 }
